to just wind down this weekend,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.550
really focus on the things
that matter for you,

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.320
your family, and your community.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.350
So the secret consulting technique

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.440
I'm talking about in this video

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.880
is the power of the question "So what?"

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.150
The power of the "So what?"

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.180
So why is this a question
that is extremely powerful

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.840
and helpful in your work, not
only if you're a consultant,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.460
but also working in any
other professional job?

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.293
And in order to understand

00:01:44.293 --> 00:01:46.800
what is so special about this question,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.370
let's first dig in to what a typical day

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.480
in a corporate environment looks like.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.160
You will have lots of
meetings with colleagues

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.760
from your department or
from other department.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.150
You will receive data.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.930
You will then conduct
analysis based on the data.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.950
You will then probably prepare the results

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.300
in some kind of documents,
whether it's in Word,

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.960
PowerPoint, or maybe
even an Excel analysis.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.410
And then in one way or another,

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.090
you will discuss this
work with your supervisor,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.480
with your team lead,
with other colleagues,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.810
and all of them reiterate,
and then, finally,

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.000
come to a result that's beneficial

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.890
to whatever task you were working on.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.080
So while being a project lead at McKinsey,

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.780
I noticed that there
is one thing that many,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.910
especially of the more junior
colleagues, struggle with,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.970
and this is really
understand what the purpose

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.460
of a certain task they
were asked to do is.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.490
What this leads to then

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.920
is that this junior colleague
will work on a certain task,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.700
and he will think that it's
completed, and it's just fine,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.780
because he did exactly
what he was asked to do,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.970
but then when you review
it, when you look into it,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.450
it might even be the case that,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.570
yes, this was what you're asked to do,

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.300
but then this activity
that in the end turned out

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.720
to not really solve the question at hand,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.730
to not really provide answers

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.540
to the issues that
you're actually raising.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.530
But the junior consultant didn't realize

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.430
because he didn't really understand

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.840
why he was doing a specific thing,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.210
but he just tried to
follow the instructions

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.247
as closely as possible because
probably he has in his head,

00:03:14.247 --> 00:03:16.766
"Well, if I'm asked to
do this specific thing

00:03:16.766 --> 00:03:19.070
"and then if I do this specific
thing, then it's okay."

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.420
But frequently you find out

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.500
that while you're doing a certainly thing,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.880
even though initially you were asked

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.190
to do it in a certain way,

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.050
this way just doesn't make sense.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:27.883
You will also need to maybe look

00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:30.270
at traditional data and combine it,

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.700
maybe conduct another interview

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.670
that you didn't plan to do beforehand.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.570
But then instead of
calling the other colleague

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.830
and asking the questions that
obviously would be needed

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.415
in order to really solve the problem,

00:03:40.415 --> 00:03:41.630
you just don't do it

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.360
because this is not what
your direct supervisor

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.660
or your team lead asked you initially

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.190
when giving you a specific task.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:50.506
And, of course, this is
subpar because by doing that,

00:03:50.506 --> 00:03:52.490
the iterations on your work needed,

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.210
you will need to go back to the desk,

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.560
then work on it again,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.180
then maybe compile the additional data,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.510
the additional information.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.590
Potentially you repeat certain activities

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:01.910
to then only review it again.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.700
So how does the question of the "So what?"

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:05.960
come into play here?

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.470
And what's important to understand here

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.100
is that the main objective of consultants

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.480
is to derive insights,
to derive conclusions

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.030
from analysis that you conduct

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:18.120
that have a real business value

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.630
for the company you work for.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.170
One of the biggest arguments

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.610
that you can get from your team lead

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.200
or maybe from partner
of your consultant firm

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.280
after you complete an analysis

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.737
is that this person says to you,

00:04:29.737 --> 00:04:32.890
"Wow, thank you, that
was really insightful."

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:36.280
Being insightful is one of the best things

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.410
somebody can say about you in
such a corporate environment

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.760
because this means that
you helped the person

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.690
really come to a conclusion

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:46.490
that he otherwise would not have derived.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.350
It means that your analysis
is so full of insights,

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.890
full of valuable conclusions,

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.200
that it really provide a value

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.760
to the people you are working for.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.920
So what does this have
to do with a consultant

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.730
who just followed order
but didn't really come up

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.470
with a great result and
answers to questions?

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.650
Well, what this means
is that this consultant

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.670
did not really derive the
insights by doing his work

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.680
that he was suppose to come up with.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.040
His work was not really insightful

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.990
even though he might have followed exactly

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.320
the things he was asked to do.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.540
And consultants use one test to understand

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.890
whether your analysis, whether your work,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.720
was insightful or not,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.810
and it's a bit of a thought experiment,

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.040
and it asks you to
imagine yourself sitting

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.830
in a large boardroom with
all the C-level executives

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.440
of the company you're working for,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.680
and then you present your results,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.107
and then the CEO of the company asks you,

00:05:40.107 --> 00:05:42.967
"What is the 'So what?'
of what you are doing?

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:45.690
"What is the 'So what?' Of your analysis?"

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.887
And these are just some
other words for asking,

00:05:47.887 --> 00:05:49.677
"Well, what are the insights?

00:05:49.677 --> 00:05:53.077
"What do I learn from your
analysis for my company?

00:05:53.077 --> 00:05:56.077
"What conclusions can I
draw from your analysis

00:05:56.077 --> 00:05:58.390
"for the things, the problems,
that I want to solve?"

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.840
The principle of the "So what?"

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.900
is so powerful that it is both useful,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.820
on the one hand side,
for your communication,

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.290
both oral and written communication,

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:11.250
but also for how you structure your work

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.930
on a day-to-day basis.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.040
So let's dive into the communication.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.580
As you know, especially if
you're a visitor of my channel,

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:19.870
in corporate environments,

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:23.400
very frequently you will use PowerPoint

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.430
to visualize and structure
the results of your work.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.130
And from very own experience,

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.440
I can tell you that
there is a big tendency

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.900
if you receive lots of
data as a consultant,

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.250
and then if you do
analyze and organize it,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.700
there's a big natural tendency for you

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.610
to then just create lots of
PowerPoint pages out of it

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.500
because you want to show
some results of your work.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:46.000
You put lots of time into
working with the data

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.010
so there also needs to be

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.990
some visible output out of there, right?

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.000
And this leads to then
creating a large document

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.230
of chart after chart, after chart,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:56.740
visualizing what customer
structure the companies have,

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.270
the geographies the
companies is working in,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.200
all kinds of cuts of the data

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.340
that you then prepare
for your team to look at.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:06.130
But now as a project lead,

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:09.010
the question that you ask is "So what?"

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:10.330
So dear analyst,

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.960
dear associate now working
for me here on my team,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.060
it's great that you look
into all these things

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.210
so that you prepared
this very big document,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.800
but what are the so whats

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.400
that we can take out of your analysis?

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.290
This is then where especially
junior consultants struggle,

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.130
and then their work falls short

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.140
of what is actually needed and expected

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.600
because they created some
potentially great-looking pages.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.080
They spent lots of time
analyzing all the data

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.310
that they get from the client,

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:41.710
but all this work did not
lead to any tangible so whats.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.210
It didn't lead to any valuable
and actionable insights

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:46.620
that the client can achieve.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.150
So what can you do to make sure

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.680
that both your oral and
written communication

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.640
always contains a strong "So what?"

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.070
And especially in written communication,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.720
if you work in PowerPoint,

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:01.400
the so called action
title is the main tool

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.160
to use in order to convey your "So what?"

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.420
The action title should precisely

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.310
contain the "So what?" of
the page that you create.

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:11.710
And if you haven't seen it yet,

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:15.110
I created a video explaining
what an action title is

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.720
and how you come up with a good,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.910
strong, powerful action title.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.430
I will link it above
for you to check it out.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.260
If you have not seen it yet,
I can highly recommend it.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.020
And if you're really serious to learn

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.160
how to create slide
presentations in the same way

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.000
that large strategy
consulting firms do it,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.040
I created a whole
multi-hour course on that.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.590
I will leave a link in
the description below.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.220
I have over 10,000
students taking the course

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.730
with lots of reviews for you to check out,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.470
so have a look whether
this might be something

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:45.640
that might add value to you.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.590
And besides the communication,

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.840
this powerful question of the "So what?"

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.940
can also help you guide and
prioritize your daily work.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.370
Especially as a project leader,

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:57.370
it was my responsibility to lead the team,

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.620
the different associates
I was working with,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.640
towards insights to what results

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.570
that were really helpful for the clients.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:05.510
To achieve this,

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.470
we often had joint
problem solving questions,

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.687
asking ourselves, "What
are the things to do next?

00:09:11.687 --> 00:09:14.090
"What analysis do we now want to do?"

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:16.117
and, "What do we hope to get out of it?

00:09:16.117 --> 00:09:17.487
"What so whats?

00:09:17.487 --> 00:09:20.220
"What implications do we
hope to get out of them?"

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.400
So this time is well spent
towards finding answers,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.880
finding solutions for the
problems we were asked to solve.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.537
So next time you work on
something, ask yourself,

00:09:29.537 --> 00:09:31.677
"What problem am I really solving?

00:09:31.677 --> 00:09:34.117
"Am I really creating insights here

00:09:34.117 --> 00:09:36.057
"for the company I'm working for?

00:09:36.057 --> 00:09:38.137
"What value can I add?

00:09:38.137 --> 00:09:41.400
"What is the 'So what?' of my work?"

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.580
Ask yourself the questions, "So what?"

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.100
When you just created a
document, an analysis,

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:47.560
what do you get out of it?

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.660
What is the implication of
the things that you are doing?

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.240
So I sure hope that this video

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.810
had some good and
tangible so whats for you,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.460
that there are some clear takeaways

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.870
and learnings for you
after watching this video.

